{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## RCA — root cause PROVEN at runtime (not the reported hypothesis)\n\nThe ticket's own hypothesis was \"the MCP server is launched from a global/stale Python env\nthat lacks the `[agents]` Bedrock extra\". That is **wrong in an important way**: there is no\nstale env, and `[agents]` has never carried Bedrock. The server runs from a container whose\ndependency set is built exactly as specified — and the specification omits the provider.\n\n### The mechanism\n\n1. `pyproject.toml:131` — `[agents]` is `pydantic-ai-slim[anthropic,retries,duckduckgo]>=1.107,<2`.\n   It carries **no** bedrock support, by design.\n2. `pyproject.toml:142-144` — `[bedrock]` is a **separate, opt-in** extra\n   (`pydantic-ai-slim[bedrock]>=1,<2`). The split is deliberate: commit `747e14945593`\n   (ticket 2932) states \"boto3 stays out of the default install: `[agents]` is unchanged and\n   the new `[bedrock]` extra is opt-in, with dev carrying it\".\n3. `pyproject.toml:213+` — `[dev]` pulls boto3/botocore, which is why the repo `.venv` works\n   and masked this in every local session.\n4. `infra/compose/Dockerfile.mcp:43` — the deployed image builds with\n   `uv sync --locked --no-dev --extra agents --extra mcp`. **No `--extra bedrock`, and\n   `--no-dev`**, so the container has neither source of the provider.\n5. `rebar.toml:198-199` — the configured model is `bedrock:us.anthropic.claude-opus-4-8`.\n   Because the container sets `REBAR_ROOT=/var/gerrit/site/mcp-code`, config discovery is\n   rooted there (`src/rebar/_config_sources.py:74-104`, `:188-217`), and that clone carries\n   this same `rebar.toml` — so the Bedrock model IS read.\n6. `src/rebar/llm/bedrock_model.py:127-139` — the provider builder imports\n   `pydantic_ai.providers.bedrock` lazily and raises the named `LLMConfigError` when absent.\n   That is the exact string the ticket reports.\n\nSo: a Bedrock model is configured, the Bedrock provider package is not installed, the LLM\nlayer cannot start, `review_plan` degrades to the deterministic floor and returns an unsigned\n`INDETERMINATE` with `llm_unavailable` — minting no attestation.\n\n### Discriminating experiment (runtime, not static)\n\nTwo interpreters, **identical code, identical `LLMConfig`** (`model=\"bedrock:us.anthropic.claude-opus-4-8\"`,\n`bedrock_region_name=\"us-east-1\"`), differing only in installed extras:\n\n- venv built as `[agents,mcp]` (the image's exact extras):\n  `LLMConfigError: a bedrock model/provider is configured but the optional bedrock provider package is not installed: pip install 'pydantic-ai-slim[bedrock]'`\n  — **byte-identical to the reported `llm_error`**.\n- venv built as `[dev]` (has bedrock): `BUILT OK: BedrockProvider`.\n\nThis rules out the live alternatives: config-not-discovered (config was passed explicitly and\nidentically to both), missing credentials and missing region (both supplied; neither is\nreached — the failure is at import), and model-string/provider-qualifier parsing (identical\ninput to both).\n\n**Note on Acceptance Criterion 1:** its proposed check,\n`importlib.util.find_spec('pydantic_ai.models.bedrock')`, is a **weak oracle and would have\npassed on the broken container**. Measured in the container-equivalent venv: the module file\nships with `pydantic-ai-slim` unconditionally, so `find_spec` is truthy while `boto3` is\n`None`. The failure is raised on *import*, not on spec lookup. A correct probe imports the\nprovider (or probes `boto3`).\n\n### Defect attribution\n\n`caused_by` -> **fd4a-4e91-f179-4932** (commit `8297083532a3`, 2026-08-24, \"Add rebar-mcp HTTP\nservice to the AWS box\"), the change that introduced `infra/compose/Dockerfile.mcp` and its\n`uv sync` line.\n\nThis is deliberately **not** attributed as an unmet obligation against the Bedrock cutover.\nThe obligation (\"this repo's configured provider is Bedrock\") was created on **2026-08-03** by\ncommit `5bc3a432d728` (ticket d2ce, \"default local dev to Bedrock\"), which added\n`[llm] model = \"bedrock:...\"` to `rebar.toml`. `Dockerfile.mcp` arrived **three weeks later**,\non 2026-08-24, with the Bedrock config already in the tree — so it is the later change, it was\nauthored against the obligation, and its reviewer could plausibly have caught the omission.\nThe date test therefore points at fd4a, not d2ce.\n\nClose class: `plan_defect` — the image's dependency specification was wrong at authoring time,\nnot broken later by someone else.\n\n### Legitimacy: `defect` (cited)\n\n`docs/adr/0104-mcp-on-box.md:98` designs this server to mint **both** op-cert kinds\n(\"plan-review at claim, completion-verifier at close\"), and `Dockerfile.mcp:36` declares the\n`agents` extra to be \"the rebar.llm gate runtime the certified tools drive\", with compose\nsetting `REBAR_MCP_ALLOW_LLM=1`. An image that cannot start the configured provider cannot\nmint a plan-review op-cert, so it violates a cited design contract.\n\n### Second, independent gap found on the same surface (would make the fix fail differently)\n\nAdding the extra alone is **not sufficient** for the deployed server. The `mcp` compose\nservice (`infra/compose/docker-compose.yml:319+`) and the blue-green\n`mcp_run_new` (`infra/scripts/autodeploy.sh:367-396`) set **no AWS region at all**, while the\nsibling `review-bot` service sets both `REBAR_LLM_BEDROCK_REGION` and `AWS_DEFAULT_REGION`\nwith a MEASURED comment (`docker-compose.yml:143-162`, ticket a574) recording that\nin-container `boto3.session.Session().region_name` is `None` and bedrock-runtime construction\nraises `NoRegionError` — and that this is independent of IMDS, which resolves fine (ticket\n9249). `src/rebar/llm/bedrock_model.py:160-173` is the corresponding no-region raise.\n\n### Credentials / region finding (verified read-only against AWS, not assumed)\n\n- Instance `i-00880b2c7f13527c5` is in **us-east-1**, instance profile\n  `rebar-gerrit-instance-profile` -> role `rebar-gerrit-instance-role`.\n- That role carries inline policy `rebar-gerrit-bedrock-converse`, allowing\n  `bedrock:InvokeModel` + `bedrock:InvokeModelWithResponseStream` on\n  `arn:aws:bedrock:*::foundation-model/anthropic.claude-*` and\n  `arn:aws:bedrock:*:896586841071:inference-profile/*.anthropic.claude-*`.\n\nSo **credentials and permissions are already in place** and reach the container via the\ninstance role. Region is the gap, and it is a config/env gap, not a credential one."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1787868333193026001,"uuid":"b293e605-77d5-4790-b577-15fc120dc553"}